Session-scoped cookie deletion rules (FEATURES 3.3; Phase 4)

The cookie-banner blocker shipped as part of the privacy baseline; the other
half of FEATURES 3.3 -- "delete after session" -- never did. This adds it, as
a Cookies and site data group in the Privacy Center: a delete-on-close switch,
and per-site rules with three states.

Session-only is the interesting one. It deletes that site's cookies at quit
regardless of the global switch, which is what you want for a site you have to
sign into but do not want remembered. "Keep" is its mirror: it survives the
delete-on-close sweep, so the global switch stops being all-or-nothing and
becomes a default you can except sites from in both directions. Without both
states, "delete cookies when I close" is a setting most people turn on once,
get signed out of everything, and turn off forever.

Implemented over Gecko's existing "cookie" permission rather than a Kavacha
store, so the rules are the same ones Firefox's own machinery already honours
at shutdown, and nothing has to be taught to enforce them. The states map to
ALLOW_ACTION (1), nsICookiePermission.ACCESS_SESSION (8) and DENY_ACTION (2) --
8 rather than 3 because cookie capabilities share the value space with the
generic ones and are spaced to avoid colliding.

The delete-on-close switch needs BOTH prefs and that is the trap worth
recording:

  privacy.sanitize.sanitizeOnShutdown          master switch for the sweep
  privacy.clearOnShutdown_v2.cookiesAndStorage cookies as one of its targets

Setting either alone does nothing observable. Worse, naively clearing the
master switch when the box is unticked would silently stop clearing the user's
history and cache as well, because the same switch drives all of them. So
unticking only stands the master switch down when nothing else was relying on
it; otherwise it leaves it alone and just deselects cookies. A privacy control
that quietly disables an unrelated privacy control is exactly the failure this
pane exists to prevent.

Domains are accepted bare ("example.com") or as full URLs, normalised through
Services.io.newURI and turned into a principal from the prePath, so a pasted
deep link becomes a rule for the site rather than the page. Input that has no
host is rejected visibly instead of silently creating nothing.

Verified without a build:
  * node --check on the pane script; the pane fragment parses as well-formed
    XML under the XUL + XHTML namespaces.
  * All 68 Fluent ids referenced across the Privacy Center resolve against
    zen-preferences.ftl, and none of the newly added strings are dead.
  * ACCESS_SESSION's value, the permission type name, and both pref names were
    read out of the pinned tree (nsICookiePermission.idl:28,
    dialogs/permissions.js:217, firefox.js:1232-1238) rather than assumed.

Not verified: that cookies actually survive or vanish across a real quit. That
is a build plus two launches, and it is the arm that matters most here -- the
whole feature is a claim about what is on disk after the browser is gone.
Worth running alongside 0034's session acceptance test, which already does a
quit/relaunch cycle and checks sessionstore.jsonlz4.
